📘 LeetCode 417: Pacific Atlantic Water Flow — Interview Notes
1. Core Intuition
❌ Brute Force

Start DFS/BFS from every cell and check whether it can reach both oceans.

Repeats the same paths many times.
Time Complexity: O((m × n)²) in the worst case.
✅ Optimized Thinking (Reverse DFS)

Instead of asking:

"Can this cell reach the ocean?"

Ask:

"Starting from the ocean, which cells can reach me if I reverse the flow?"

Reverse the water flow.

Normal water flow:

5 → 4 → 3 → 2 → Ocean

Reverse DFS:

Ocean → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5

Move from lower/equal height to higher/equal height.

2. Reverse DFS Rule

Normal flow:

neighbor <= current

Reverse DFS:

neighbor >= current

This is the most important observation in the problem.

3. Starting Points
Pacific Ocean

Touches:

Top row
Left column
P P P P
P . . .
P . . .
P . . .

Run DFS from every Pacific border cell.

Atlantic Ocean

Touches:

Bottom row
Right column
. . . A
. . . A
. . . A
A A A A

Run DFS from every Atlantic border cell.

4. Two Visited Sets

Maintain two sets:

pacificVisited
atlanticVisited

After DFS:

Pacific reachable cells

+

Atlantic reachable cells

Answer:

Pacific ∩ Atlantic

Take the intersection, not the union.

5. DFS Logic

For every DFS call:

Enter DFS
      ↓
Base Case
      ↓
Mark Visited
      ↓
Explore Up
      ↓
Explore Down
      ↓
Explore Left
      ↓
Explore Right
      ↓
Return (Backtrack)
6. Base Cases

Stop DFS if:

Out of bounds
Already visited
Neighbor height is smaller than current height (reverse DFS condition fails)
7. Recursive Mental Model

Always think like the computer.

For every recursive call, ask:

Which cell are we visiting?
Does the base case trigger?
What changes?
Which neighbor is checked next?
Does a recursive call happen?
What does the call stack look like?
When do we backtrack?
8. Call Stack Example
DFS(0,0)
│
└── DFS(0,1)
      │
      └── DFS(1,1)

When DFS(1,1) finishes:

DFS(0,0)
│
└── DFS(0,1)

Execution continues from where it paused.

It does not

Restart the function ❌
Return immediately ❌

It continues with the next neighbor.

9. Backtracking

A DFS function returns only after:

Up
Down
Left
Right

have all been processed.

Backtracking happens because:

The function has finished all its work.

Not because of the base case.

10. Why is visited Necessary?

Equal heights create cycles.

Example:

2 2

Reverse DFS:

A ↔ B

Without visited:

DFS(A)
 ↓
DFS(B)
 ↓
DFS(A)
 ↓
DFS(B)
 ...

Infinite recursion.

11. Important Observation

Strictly increasing heights:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4

cannot create cycles because

4 → 3

is not allowed in reverse DFS.

Only equal heights can create cycles.

12. Overall Algorithm
Create Pacific visited set

Create Atlantic visited set

Run DFS from:
    Top row
    Left column

Store in Pacific

Run DFS from:
    Bottom row
    Right column

Store in Atlantic

Return:
Pacific ∩ Atlantic
13. Complexity
Time
O(m × n)

Each cell is visited at most once for Pacific and once for Atlantic.

Space
O(m × n)
Two visited sets
Recursion stack
14. Common Mistakes

❌ Running DFS from every cell

❌ Forgetting to reverse the direction

❌ Using

neighbor <= current

instead of

neighbor >= current

❌ Forgetting visited

❌ Returning the union instead of the intersection

15. Interview Explanation (30 Seconds)

"Instead of starting DFS from every cell, which repeats many paths, I reverse the problem. I start DFS from the Pacific borders (top row and left column) and mark every cell that can reach the Pacific by moving only to neighbors with height greater than or equal to the current cell. I repeat the same process for the Atlantic borders (bottom row and right column). Finally, I return the intersection of the two visited sets because those are the cells that can reach both oceans."

